# Every version and checksum the hcw-lab image is built from, one per line.
#
# The Dockerfile copies this file in and sources it at the top of each RUN,
# so bumping a line here and pushing is the whole update procedure: CI
# rebuilds both targets, runs smoke.sh, and publishes. Nothing in the
# Dockerfiles repeats a version, with one exception noted at BASE_DIGEST.
#
# Every download is verified against the SHA256 beside it before it is used.
# The sums were resolved on 2026-09-25 from the publisher's own checksum file
# (releases.hashicorp.com SHA256SUMS, kubeconform CHECKSUMS, get.helm.sh
# .sha256sum, dl.k8s.io .sha256, PyPI's JSON API, the Terraform registry's
# download endpoint) and, for the GitHub source tarballs, computed locally
# from the downloaded archive. Every version was the newest stable release its
# publisher listed on that date (for the second azurerm mirror, the newest
# 4.x). Values are plain shell assignments; the file
# is read by /bin/sh inside the build and must stay that way (no export, no
# expansions, no spaces around '=').
#
# 2026-10-14 (#1133, the weekly image scan): terraform, azurerm 5.x, azapi
# and the Azure CLI moved to their publishers' newest releases, the sums read
# the same ways that day. The other mirrored providers and tools the scan
# named (azurerm 4.x, alz, random, modtm, time, kubeconform, helm, kubectl)
# were already at their newest release; their findings are Go library
# versions only a publisher's rebuild can move, and the weekly scan says when.
#
# 2026-10-31 (#1426 again): the base moved to python:3.14.10-slim-trixie,
# terraform to 1.17.1, kubectl to 1.37.2 and azurerm 5.x to 5.12.0, the sums
# read the same ways that day; AZURE_CLI_PYTHON_OVERRIDES is new and says why.
#
# 2026-10-31, later (#1458, the Python 3.15 floor): the base moved to
# python:3.15.2-slim-trixie and ansible-core to the 2.22 line, with its
# dependencies re-resolved for CPython 3.15.
#
# 2026-11-02: ansible-core 2.22.0 final replaced its release candidate
# (the wheel's SHA256 read from PyPI's JSON API and checked against the
# downloaded file), and BASE_DIGEST moved with the patch tag once its
# riscv64 build landed.
#
# 2026-11-03 (#1505): PyYAML's wheel is built from its source archive in the
# fetch stage and published for the lab host; nothing is blocked. The
# ansible-core block and the PyYAML block below say how.
#
# 2026-11-03 (#1519, the weekly image-tools scan): the Azure CLI moved to
# 2.92.0 and alz to 0.22.1, the sums read the same ways that day. Azure CLI
# 2.92.0 ships pip 26.2.2 itself, so its AZURE_CLI_PYTHON_OVERRIDES line
# went, as the full build's check asked.

# Base image, on every FROM in lab-image/Dockerfile: the Docker Official
# Image python:3.15.2-slim-trixie, CPython 3.15.2 built on
# debian:trixie-slim (Debian 13, point release 13.7: /etc/debian_version in
# that image on 2026-10-31). The full tag is written because the tag beside
# a digest is documentation and this one names the patch release. 3.15 is
# the newest release line, adopted once its N-2 patch (3.15.2) existed, the
# rule in scripts/version-floors.json (#1458).
#
# Resolved 2026-11-02. On 2026-10-31 the patch tag and the line tag
# python:3.15-slim-trixie had different index digests (an index is the list
# of per-platform images behind one tag): the line tag also listed a riscv64
# image, still the 3.15.1 build, which the patch tag did not list yet, and
# the pin was the patch tag's digest of that day
# (sha256:c4158543ecb725b99e57d129dfd43d761d4c43e4577b5e23326aabb223cf7f4e).
# By 2026-11-02 the riscv64 3.15.2 build had landed and the two tags list
# the same eight images under the same index digest, which is the pin now,
# read from Docker Hub's Docker-Content-Digest header and from
# mirror.gcr.io, the two equal and each body hashed to it. The linux/amd64
# image, the only one this build uses, is the same manifest on both days
# (sha256:3053ea19f29305df68292b41291d621e0cbc9c1c826822c631713891ea0b64e9),
# and its config sets PYTHON_VERSION=3.15.2. A move like that is harmless,
# because the pin is a digest and does not follow the tag: the weekly
# image-base bump opens one ordinary pull request to the new digest, with
# the same linux/amd64 image inside, and then stops, so it does not loop.
#
# One base for every stage, the build-only fetch, vendor and mirror stages
# included, rather than debian:trixie-slim for the stages that need no
# Python: the python image IS debian:trixie-slim plus CPython (its linux/amd64
# manifest names debian:trixie-slim's own amd64 digest as its base), so the
# saving would be about 40 MB in stages that never ship, against a second
# digest to keep in step; and vendor-avm.sh then runs at build time on the
# same Python the runner ships instead of Debian's python3 (3.13 in
# trixie). No Debian python3 package is installed anywhere in the image.
#
# The FROM lines carry this same reference, because a FROM cannot read a
# file; the publish workflow fails the build if any FROM that is not an
# earlier stage differs from BASE_IMAGE@BASE_DIGEST. The digest is the image
# INDEX digest, from the registry's Docker-Content-Digest header (the method
# vps-agent/lib/capabilities.js documents), cross-checked with
# `docker buildx imagetools inspect`, which reported the same value.
# BASE_PYTHON_VERSION is the CPython release that digest carries; smoke.sh
# compares `python3 --version` with it, so a digest bump that moves Python
# fails until this line moves too. The owner's floor is the newest 3.x line
# at no more than two patch releases behind its latest
# (scripts/version-floors.json holds the numbers).
BASE_IMAGE=python:3.15.2-slim-trixie
BASE_DIGEST=sha256:8c13a3f37a6a5b71498228e902c4b9f51c1f7900d49fabf71f9a650c6120e0d0
BASE_PYTHON_VERSION=3.15.2

# Base of lab-image/sandbox-template/Dockerfile, Docker's Claude Code sandbox
# template (Ubuntu 26.04.1 LTS "resolute" on 2026-09-25). Same rule and same
# check as BASE_IMAGE; resolved the same two ways, and unchanged since the
# recipe landed with #676.
SANDBOX_BASE_IMAGE=docker/sandbox-templates:claude-code
SANDBOX_BASE_DIGEST=sha256:30ec2b1bb76e3993aff912c553a5c249ad95b595249a32fde7158eafb71bda25

# Tools in the runner target.
TERRAFORM_VERSION=1.17.1
TERRAFORM_SHA256=4638628058e71a55212b1c563712eac03b902128f40740f3dc8174df12135061
KUBECONFORM_VERSION=0.8.0
KUBECONFORM_SHA256=9bc2bffbf71f261128533edaf912153948b7ff238f9a531ae6d34466ec287883
HELM_VERSION=4.3.1
HELM_SHA256=0403a88ae20913d11b10a2467b50087721fea7581736da6b9d3992f5357fe54b
# ansible-core 2.22.0, the first release line that runs on CPython 3.15,
# released 2026-11-02 on the roadmap's date
# (https://docs.ansible.com/projects/ansible-core/devel/roadmap/ROADMAP_2_22.html,
# read 2026-10-31; the wheel reached PyPI at 17:35 UTC). 2.21 cannot be
# used on 3.15: its controller range is Python 3.12 to 3.14, and 2.21.4
# stops at start-up on 3.15.2 with `AttributeError: module 'dataclasses'
# has no attribute '_is_type'` (measured 2026-10-31). 2.22 adds 3.15 and
# drops 3.12 on the controller, while dropping 3.9 on the target waits for
# 2.23, so the lab host's module side (Ubuntu's 3.12 or 3.14) stays in
# range. Its wheel declares Requires-Python >=3.13 and the same five
# requirements as 2.21.5 (read from the wheel's own METADATA on
# 2026-11-02). The SHA256 is the wheel's, from PyPI's JSON API and equal
# to the downloaded file's. Until 2026-11-02 this line was 2.22.0rc2, so
# that everything else for CPython 3.15 could be prepared.
# These three ANSIBLE_CORE_* lines are also the lab
# host's: lab-host/bootstrap.sh keeps no copy and installs ansible-core from
# them with --require-hashes (LAB-13, #959). The weekly
# `chore/lab-pins-image-base` pull request moves them within their release
# line; a new line is moved by hand.
ANSIBLE_CORE_VERSION=2.22.0
ANSIBLE_CORE_SHA256=8285d4cc81c3e3eb2d954e75531724fe0ae154d228475638123451f034a74657
# ansible-core's dependencies and theirs, each the newest release inside the
# range its dependant declares, as the one wheel pip selects for CPython 3.15
# on linux x86_64 (cryptography publishes several manylinux x86_64 wheels;
# this is the cp315-abi3.abi3t-manylinux_2_34 one). Resolved 2026-10-31 by
# `pip download --only-binary=:all:` on python:3.15.2-slim-trixie itself,
# each hash equal to PyPI's JSON API for that file.
#
# PyYAML: 6.0.3, its newest release, publishes no wheel for CPython 3.15, and
# only a new PyYAML release can (PyPI rejects new files on a release older
# than 14 days; yaml/pyyaml#951). The pyyaml line below therefore carries its
# cp314 wheel's hash, which nothing on 3.15 installs: the fetch stage sets the
# line aside and builds the wheel from PyYAML's source archive (the PyYAML
# block further down, #1505), and the lab host replaces the line with the
# published wheel's hash from PYYAML_WHEEL_SHA256. The line stays so that the
# block still lists every dependency ansible-core declares, which the pin
# bot's test checks. Every other line is the cp315 wheel pip selects. One per
# line in pip's requirements syntax. The fetch stage downloads them, and the
# runner installs them, in pip's hash-checking mode: --require-hashes refuses
# a wheel whose SHA256 differs, and also refuses to resolve any dependency
# that is not listed here with a hash, so a new transitive dependency fails
# the build instead of arriving unpinned.
ANSIBLE_CORE_DEPS='
jinja2==3.1.6 --hash=sha256:85ece4451f492d0c13c5dd7c13a64681a86afae63a5f347908daf103ce6d2f67
markupsafe==3.0.4 --hash=sha256:925f929d6b59a8b3f8b8c6ac363cd0af7eecc81efb3071770b3c6717c450a369
pyyaml==6.0.3 --hash=sha256:c458b6d084f9b935061bc36216e8a69a7e293a2f1e68bf956dcd9e6cbcd143f5
cryptography==50.0.2 --hash=sha256:e105ab60406787da31fccc883fc0f733af1efd78f0136a4599692c4083a73d0c
cffi==2.1.1 --hash=sha256:aeae0e330c9f6acd681f647d46cefd30c29f93e3392882e792e82080c9691399
pycparser==3.12 --hash=sha256:a076f3f489b39b43ed13563a1dafe42447445bec2e294154ec5c57fcdda4c85e
packaging==26.3 --hash=sha256:d7193f7c8e4e93f444fde0262bf90af30e16fa0ad0ad44cb553c87339b23cd1c
resolvelib==1.2.1 --hash=sha256:fb06b66c8da04172d9e72a21d7d06186d8919e32ae5ab5cdf5b9d920be805ac2
'

# --- PyYAML built from source: a temporary block (#1505, 2026-11-02) -------
#
# Why: ansible-core imports PyYAML, and the image installs wheels only
# (--only-binary=:all: above). PyYAML 6.0.3, its newest release, publishes no
# wheel for CPython 3.15, and PyPI no longer accepts new files on a release
# older than 14 days, so only a new PyYAML release can carry one
# (https://github.com/yaml/pyyaml/issues/951). Rather than wait for it, the
# image's fetch stage builds PyYAML's wheel itself from the publisher's
# source archive on PyPI, verified by the SHA256 below, and the runner then
# installs that wheel in the same offline, hash-checked step as every other
# wheel. So the thing trusted is PyPI's source archive instead of PyPI's
# wheel. This works on any Python the base carries (3.14 today, 3.15 next),
# so the move to 3.15 (#1468) does not wait on PyYAML.
#
# This block is temporary: it is deleted the day PyYAML publishes a wheel
# for BASE_PYTHON_VERSION, and the pyyaml line in ANSIBLE_CORE_DEPS then goes
# back to being the one the image installs. Everything that goes with it,
# in the image, the host, the workflows, the tests and the docs, is listed
# once, in docs/runbooks/pyyaml-wheel.md under "Deleting it when PyYAML
# ships a wheel"; nothing else keeps its own list.
#
# The pyyaml line in ANSIBLE_CORE_DEPS stays, because lab-host/bootstrap.sh
# reads that block and installs PyPI's wheel on the host, where the cp314
# wheel still exists today; the Dockerfile sets that line aside and writes
# the built wheel's own hash in its place in the runner's requirements file.
# The host side is the built wheel published as the pyyaml-<version>-cp315
# release asset, pinned in PYYAML_WHEEL_SHA256 at the end of this block.
# scripts/lab-image-pyyaml-wheel.test.mjs holds the two versions equal and
# checks this block's shape.
#
# PYYAML_SDIST_SHA256 is the SHA256 of pyyaml-<version>.tar.gz, the source
# archive, from PyPI's JSON API (https://pypi.org/pypi/pyyaml/6.0.3/json,
# `digests.sha256` of the file whose packagetype is sdist), read 2026-11-02.
# PYYAML_BUILD_DEPS are the tools the build needs, as hash-checked wheels in
# the same syntax as ANSIBLE_CORE_DEPS. PyYAML 6.0.3's pyproject.toml asks
# for setuptools and Cython>=3.0 on Python 3.13 and later, and its build
# backend lives in its own packaging/ directory, so the Dockerfile installs
# these first and builds with --no-build-isolation. With that flag alone pip
# would use whatever is installed without reading the pyproject's asks;
# --check-build-dependencies, passed as well, makes pip compare the two and
# fail if a tool is missing or too old. A line may carry several --hash
# values; pip accepts a file matching any one of them, so the one cython
# line serves the base's Python today and after #1468, and pip takes the
# compiled wheel when one matches. Sums from PyPI's JSON API on 2026-11-02
# (cython 3.3.1, setuptools 84.2.1, wheel 0.48.0, each the newest release
# that day). cython's three hashes, in the order written on its line:
#   1. cython-3.3.1-cp314-cp314-manylinux2014_x86_64.manylinux_2_17_x86_64.manylinux_2_28_x86_64.whl
#   2. cython-3.3.1-cp315-cp315-manylinux2014_x86_64.manylinux_2_17_x86_64.manylinux_2_28_x86_64.whl
#   3. cython-3.3.1-py3-none-any.whl (pure Python: any CPython, slower to compile with)
#
# The C library the wheel needs, libyaml, comes from Debian's libyaml-dev in
# the fetch stage and is linked in statically: the base image carries no
# libyaml shared library, so a wheel that linked it dynamically would import
# without the C loader in the runner. The fetch stage fails the build if the
# built extension links libyaml dynamically or if yaml.__with_libyaml__ is
# false, and smoke.sh checks the shipped image the same way. The build step
# itself runs with no network at all.
PYYAML_VERSION=6.0.3
PYYAML_SDIST_SHA256=d76623373421df22fb4cf8817020cbb7ef15c725b9d5e45f17e189bfc384190f
PYYAML_BUILD_DEPS='
cython==3.3.1 --hash=sha256:7a6c191617853f2e0d5936c73f5d5cd096548f4d9e0fa37612ef721591c42cb3 --hash=sha256:fcb6c86dddaedd1da5d77ba2469c1a4bea52442e02d8f0653ff78667fbaaa8d9 --hash=sha256:61abc7feedd20dac6c4ff7ab3a30e17136a067b242ab9253e2b4a6d9a72c2b61
setuptools==84.2.1 --hash=sha256:ca140b45d5325a9478f880e351484f62fef15f336462e449ca0c03c873d589c2
wheel==0.48.0 --hash=sha256:3217dcc807155e45db462d7ef2431f5ddda0d7273b700d05a67b271ceb1287ab
'
# PYYAML_WHEEL_SHA256 is for the lab host, which builds nothing from source:
# the SHA256 of the wheel publish-pyyaml-wheel.yml built from the same
# archive for BASE_PYTHON_VERSION's line and attached to this repository's
# release pyyaml-<PYYAML_VERSION>-cp<major><minor> (the workflow's step
# summary prints the line to paste here). lab-host/bootstrap.sh downloads
# that wheel, checks it against this value and its attestation with cosign,
# and installs it in place of the pyyaml line above. Empty means no wheel is
# published for this Python yet, and the host installs PyPI's wheel from
# the line above as before. One wheel serves one Python line, so the host's
# own PYTHON_VERSION (top of lab-host/bootstrap.sh) must be on the same line
# as BASE_PYTHON_VERSION while this is set; bootstrap.sh refuses to run
# otherwise, naming both. A built wheel is not byte-identical from one build
# to the next, so this is always the SHA256 of the asset on the release,
# never one recomputed from a local build. Moves by hand with PYYAML_VERSION
# and BASE_PYTHON_VERSION; the weekly bumper leaves it alone. The value is
# the pyyaml-6.0.3-cp315 release's wheel (run 41557242736, the first on main
# after the move to 3.15), checked by hand on 2026-11-03 against the
# downloaded asset and its attestation.
PYYAML_WHEEL_SHA256=fa353f42becf2bb8fd3415df15d8e0cfdac3ede206088ec1bc750f311fd8f29d
# --- end of the PyYAML block ----------------------------------------------

# Kubernetes JSON schemas for kubeconform, so the `kubeconform` capability
# validates with no network (#675): one directory of
# github.com/yannh/kubernetes-json-schema (the repository kubeconform reads
# over HTTPS by default) at a pinned commit, mirrored to
# /opt/kubeconform/schemas/<dir> under the name kubeconform's own location
# template expects. The commit is content-addressed; the SHA256 is the tree
# hash of the checked-out directory by the same pipeline AVM_CHILD_MODULES
# documents below, so a moved branch and a changed file both fail the build.
# The directory names the same Kubernetes line as KUBECTL_VERSION (1.37 for
# 1.37.2). A kubectl patch release does not move it. A new line moves both, by
# hand, in one pull request: the weekly bump cannot, because no publisher
# serves a checksum for this commit, so it says so in the weekly issue, and a
# test fails when the two sit on different lines (#1323).
KUBERNETES_JSON_SCHEMA_COMMIT=a6f9a32d2ccb64b6e4f5b41419b9c2e8ee0cce18
KUBERNETES_JSON_SCHEMA_DIR=v1.37.1-standalone-strict
KUBERNETES_JSON_SCHEMA_SHA256=fc44a2541c357219c7253e12c2667d20edf088e920123806fe48e5aa6d156fc9

# Tools added by the full target.
KUBECTL_VERSION=1.37.2
KUBECTL_SHA256=c04487523e7aa7d1493f7808174c899db93d7a9cf57531420cd76b9bd415f8f4
# Azure CLI: the upstream release, installed from Microsoft's apt repository
# as the package `azure-cli=<version>-1~<suite>` that Microsoft's install page
# documents. packages.microsoft.com publishes no trixie suite: on 2026-09-25
# https://packages.microsoft.com/repos/azure-cli/dists/ listed bookworm,
# bullseye, jammy, noble and resolute among others and no trixie, and
# https://learn.microsoft.com/cli/azure/install-azure-cli-linux?pivots=apt
# lists Debian 11 and 12 as tested and says, under "No package for your
# distribution", to use the latest Debian suite, bookworm. So the full target
# installs the bookworm build on trixie (AZURE_CLI_SUITE). That build's
# Depends (libc6 >= 2.35, libssl3 >= 3.0.0, libffi8, zlib1g, libbz2-1.0,
# libuuid1, libgcc-s1) are met by trixie's own packages, and the CLI runs on
# the Python it bundles under /opt/az, not on the image's. The sandbox
# template installs the same release from its base's own suite (resolute),
# with the same AZURE_CLI_PYTHON_OVERRIDES replacements (below).
# The apt signing key is verified by SHA256 before it is trusted; it is the
# key the bookworm and resolute suites are signed with.
AZURE_CLI_VERSION=2.92.0
AZURE_CLI_SUITE=bookworm
MICROSOFT_APT_KEY_SHA256=2fa9c05d591a1582a9aba276272478c262e95ad00acf60eaee1644d93941e3c6

# Python packages replaced inside the Azure CLI's own Python (/opt/az), one
# per line in pip's requirements syntax, each the newest release with the
# SHA256 of its one wheel (py3-none-any) from PyPI's JSON API, checked
# against the downloaded file on 2026-10-31.
#
# Why: the weekly image scan (#1426) found every CRITICAL and HIGH Python
# finding of the full image inside the Azure CLI package, not in anything
# this file pins. Azure CLI 2.91.0, the newest release in Microsoft's apt
# repository that day, bundles certifi 2024.7.4, PyJWT 2.13.0, urllib3 2.7.0
# and cryptography 48.0.1 (read from the .deb's own dist-info folders), and
# its pip 26.2.1 carries its own private copies of certifi 2026.6.17,
# msgpack 1.1.2, setuptools 70.3.0 and urllib3 2.7.0 (pip/_vendor/vendor.txt).
# The CLI's own packages accept the newer versions: msal asks for
# PyJWT[crypto] <3,>=1.0.0, requests for urllib3 <3,>=1.26, and nothing caps
# certifi. Microsoft's dev branch already moves certifi and PyJWT
# (requirements.py3.Linux.txt: certifi 2026.10.19, PyJWT 2.15.0).
#
# What stays until Microsoft releases a new CLI, and is not replaced here:
#   cryptography 48.0.1: the bundled pyOpenSSL 26.2.0 declares
#     cryptography <49,>=46.0.0, so a newer cryptography alone would break a
#     package the CLI imports. Newer pyOpenSSL releases lift that cap (26.3.0
#     asks for >=49,<50 and 26.4.0 for >=49,<51), but Microsoft still pins
#     pyOpenSSL 26.2.0 and has not tested the CLI on 26.3 or later, which
#     removed X509Req, dump_certificate_request and load_certificate_request
#     and deprecated the X509 setters the CLI's `az ad sp create-for-rbac
#     --create-cert` path uses. So neither is replaced here. Neither open
#     finding on 48.0.1 is reachable from the CLI: CVE-2026-69247 (fixed in
#     50.0.0) is in pkcs7_decrypt_der/pem/smime, which the CLI never calls,
#     and CVE-2026-69249 (fixed in 49.0.0) is slow certificate path building
#     in cryptography.x509.verification, a denial of service, which the CLI
#     does not use either (the advisories read on https://osv.dev, the
#     pyOpenSSL changelog and the CLI 2.91.0 .deb's own code on 2026-10-31).
#     Microsoft's dev branch still pins 48.0.1.
#   oauthlib 3.2.2 (a trivy scan of this build on 2026-10-31 reports
#     CVE-2026-118669, fixed in 4.0.0): a new major version, under
#     requests-oauthlib 1.2.0, and Microsoft's dev branch still pins 3.2.2.
#     The advisory is in the server-side token grant handlers, which a
#     client like the CLI does not run.
#   pip's private copies of msgpack 1.1.2, setuptools 70.3.0 and urllib3
#     2.7.0: pip 26.2.2 (below, the newest pip) still carries the same three;
#     only certifi moved in it. They wait on a pip release. pip itself stays,
#     because `az extension add` installs extensions with it.
#
# The fetch stage downloads these wheels and the full target installs them
# into /opt/az with the CLI's own Python in pip's hash-checking mode, in the
# same step that installs the CLI, then runs `pip check` there. The sandbox
# template (lab-image/sandbox-template/Dockerfile) does the same in its one
# RUN, downloading the wheels with the CLI's own pip since it has no fetch
# stage (#1473); publish-lab-image.yml builds it on every pull request that
# touches lab-image/, and lab-supply-chain.yml builds and scans it weekly,
# because nothing publishes it. Before it
# installs, the build stops if the CLI already ships a package at or above
# the version listed here (or no longer ships it at all), naming the line to
# remove: the weekly bump moves AZURE_CLI_VERSION alone, and without that
# check a newer CLI would be quietly downgraded to the older version listed.
# Remove a line once AZURE_CLI_VERSION ships that version or newer. An empty
# block, or no block at all, is fine: the fetch, the install and smoke.sh
# then skip the replacement and change nothing else.
# scripts/lab-image-azure-cli-overrides.test.mjs checks each line's shape.
AZURE_CLI_PYTHON_OVERRIDES='
certifi==2026.10.23 --hash=sha256:e9b54de76e530bb6cf0ccd036787103acb58fd42ba930ea8bf948ddbaa2f61d1
pyjwt==2.15.1 --hash=sha256:42d59d631f7768a1028a64c7ff581a9bf7519804daf91fc5b6c56e30eec5e193
urllib3==2.8.0 --hash=sha256:0cf3cae568d36aa9576b28dfb35f11328f1cb974ca7647d9475ebb86c75ac6e3
'

# Terraform providers in the filesystem mirror at /opt/terraform/mirror, each
# with the SHA256 of its linux_amd64 zip as the registry reports it.
# azurerm is mirrored twice: 5.x is current, and the AVM modules vendored
# below still constrain to ~> 4.0 / ~> 4.35. modtm and time are not
# in the issue's list but every vendored module requires them, so without
